---
import Layout from "../layouts/BasicLayout.astro";
import {METADATA, NOT_FOUND, PATCHES} from "../lib/constants";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";

/*
 * THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE OF WHAT THE HOST DOES WITHOUT IT. Cloudflare Pages answers an
 * unknown path by walking up looking for `${dir}/404.html`, and where there is none it
 * falls through to serving `/index.html` — with a 200. Measured on a real preview
 * deployment before this page was written: `/does-not-exist` and `/patches/nonsense` both
 * returned 200 and the home page, while the same two paths on the outgoing host returned
 * 404. A dead link that answers 200 is invisible to every uptime monitor, crawler and link
 * checker there is, so the whole class of broken URL would have stopped being reportable at
 * the moment the domain moved.
 *
 * Astro emits this route as `dist/404.html`, which is the file BOTH hosts look for, so one
 * page fixes the incoming host and improves the outgoing one. No redirect rule, and nothing
 * host-specific in the repository.
 *
 * ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY THE NUMBER IS A BIB, which is the only real design decision on this page.
 *
 * A 404 page's stock answers are a cartoon, a magnifying glass, or the number set huge in
 * the body face — and all three could be lifted onto any site on the internet, which is the
 * argument against them. This site already owns one distinctive object, drawn in CSS and
 * used nowhere else on the web: the race bib on `/patches`. An HTTP status code is a number
 * a reader was handed for something that is not there, and a bib is a number pinned to
 * something that is. Setting 404 as a race number states the whole thing without a caption.
 *
 * FOUR PIN HOLES, ARRIVED AT BY BUILDING THE OPPOSITE FIRST. `Patch.astro` punches four for
 * a run and two for a ride — "the count is the information" — and `.bib--booked` removes them
 * entirely, because a hole is a void in an opaque face and a booked bib has none. That
 * argument is sound, and following it gave a bib with no holes, which I built and then
 * looked at: alone on the page it reads as a rectangle with a number in it, which is exactly
 * the stock 404 this design exists to avoid. Five neighbouring bibs are what keep the wall's
 * outline treatment legible; this one has no neighbours and has to carry the shape by
 * itself. So the holes come back, painted as the edge of the punch rather than as the void —
 * the stylesheet has the mechanism and what was measured.
 *
 * WHAT WAS CUT. The first draft tagged the bib `DNS — Did Not Start`, which is precisely
 * what a results sheet prints against a number that never crossed the line, and it is the
 * best phrase in the whole subject. It is gone because on a WEB error page those three
 * letters read as Domain Name System, and a reader who takes it that way has been told
 * their network is broken. The bib now carries the number and nothing else.
 *
 * NO NEW VOCABULARY ANYWHERE. No colour that is not already a theme token, and deliberately
 * NOT `--accent`: this site spends the accent on controls and marks, and an error is
 * neither. No new typeface — the numeral wears the bib's own display treatment from
 * `Patch.astro`, which exists for exactly this, a number on a bib. And no animation: the
 * site is static and minimal, so a bib that slid or faded in would be the one moving thing
 * on the whole domain. Nothing here moves, so there is nothing for `prefers-reduced-motion`
 * to turn off.
 *
 * DRAWN IN ITS OWN SCOPED RULES RATHER THAN BY IMPORTING `Patch.astro`, which was the other
 * candidate. That component takes a `RaceEvent`, and there is no honest way to hand it one:
 * `EVENTS` is the owner's real racing history and a fake entry would reach the wall, the
 * projection and the goal cards. The shared construction is the TOKENS below — the same
 * hairline mix, the same 2px radius, the same numeral metric — which is how every other
 * component on this site stays in the family. See `BasicLayout.astro`.
 * ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * IT IS THE ONE PAGE THE BUILD-WIDE GATES CANNOT HOLD, and `tests/build-output.test.ts`
 * names it in both rather than being loosened. It is in no sitemap — `@astrojs/sitemap`
 * excludes status pages itself, so nothing here configures that — and it is reachable from
 * no link, because a page you arrive at by mistyping is not a destination anything links to.
 * Both exemptions are asserted as facts about THIS page, so a second unreachable page still
 * fails, and the 404 is separately REQUIRED to link home so a reader who lands here is not
 * stranded.
 *
 * A real Astro route on the shared layout rather than a static `public/404.html`, which is
 * what keeps it inside the theme, the analytics tag and the build-date stamp instead of
 * being a hand-maintained copy of the shell that silently rots.
 */

const title = `${NOT_FOUND.heading} — ${METADATA.name}`;
---

<Layout title={title} description={NOT_FOUND.description} noindex>
    <main class="text-[var(--text)] m-auto p-2 grid gap-2 max-w-4xl w-full sm:p-4 md:gap-3 md:p-6 lg:gap-4">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
            {/* The number is read out before the heading, and that order is deliberate rather
                 than a side effect of putting the big thing first: "404", then "Page not
                 found", then what to do about it, is a sensible sequence however a reader
                 takes it in. It is NOT `aria-hidden` — the status code is real information,
                 and hiding it would leave a screen reader user the only one not told which
                 error this is. */}
            <p class="dns-bib"><span class="dns-number">{NOT_FOUND.code}</span></p>

            <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
                {/* `break-anywhere` for the reason the wall's heading carries it: these are
                     the site's only un-clipped text columns, so their minimum intrinsic width
                     sets the document's, and a word too long for 320px scrolls the whole page
                     sideways under text zoom rather than being absorbed by a card. */}
                <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold m-0 break-anywhere">{NOT_FOUND.heading}</h1>
                <p class="text-sm max-w-[60ch] m-0">{NOT_FOUND.lede}</p>
            </div>

            {/* THE REST OF THE SITE, WHICH IS WHAT THE LEDE PROMISES. Two entries, because two
                 is genuinely all there is — the wall's own filter row takes a reader on to the
                 per-sport pages from there, and repeating those here would be a menu longer
                 than the site.

                 `text-link` on both: this codebase's idiom for a link that is a run of words,
                 and what makes them perceptible as links rather than as the prose above them.
                 A build-wide gate walks every `<a>` on every page for that.

                 ONLY THE FIRST TAKES THE GLYPH. The home link is drawn exactly like the wall's
                 way back — same arrow, same box — because they are the same object pointing
                 the same way. The second is not a way back to anywhere, and an arrow-left
                 beside it would say it was; `text-link` alone is what this site already uses
                 for a link that is a run of words, as each role card's company name is.
                 `min-h-6` on both is the 1.5rem floor this codebase set for a small link,
                 font-relative so it grows with the reader's text. */}
            <ul class="flex flex-col gap-2 list-none m-0 p-0">
                <li>
                    <a href="/" class="text-link inline-flex items-center gap-2 min-h-6 text-sm">
                        <span class={iconClass(NOT_FOUND.home_icon)} aria-hidden="true"></span>{NOT_FOUND.home_label}
                    </a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a href="/patches" class="text-link inline-flex items-center min-h-6 text-sm">{PATCHES.heading}</a>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>

<style>
    /*
     * THE BIB, IN THE ONE TREATMENT THAT FITS: no face, hairline edge, normal-polarity ink —
     * `.bib--booked`'s shape in `Patch.astro`, reached by the same reasoning rather than by
     * copying a class. A solid bib is one you have earned; an outline is one still ahead of
     * you; a page that does not exist is neither, and of the two the outline is the honest
     * one, because the face is what a bib gets when there is a race behind it.
     *
     * THE VALUES ARE COPIED, AND NOTHING LINKS THEM. An earlier version of this comment said
     * the 32% hairline and the 2px radius "are `.bib--booked`'s own, so a re-tone moves both
     * together" — which is false twice over: they are literals duplicated by hand, and a
     * re-tone of `Patch.astro` moves nothing here. It also claimed the pin holes below use
     * "the same 7px inset", and they do not: the wall uses 7px, this uses 9px, because this
     * bib's padding is larger. Both claims were caught by a review panel, and a wrong comment
     * is worse than no comment because it stops the next reader checking.
     *
     * What IS shared by construction is the theme tokens — `--text`, and through it the
     * hairline mix — which live in `BasicLayout.astro` and are the site-wide mechanism every
     * component uses. Extracting the bib into something both files import was considered and
     * rejected: `Patch.astro` takes a `RaceEvent`, and `EVENTS` is the owner's real racing
     * history, so there is no honest fake to hand it. Accept the duplication, or make the bib
     * a component that takes primitives — but do not believe these two re-tone together.
     */
    .dns-bib {
        container-type: inline-size;
        width: min(22rem, 100%);
        margin: 0;
        /* A bib is a landscape rectangle and the number is centred on it — that proportion
           and that centring are most of what makes the shape legible as a bib at a glance.
           The wall can leave its number top-left because five neighbours establish what the
           object is; this one is alone and has to say so by itself. */
        padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
        display: grid;
        place-items: center;
        border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 32%, transparent);
        border-radius: 2px;
        color: var(--text);
        /*
         * FOUR PIN HOLES, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS READS AS A BIB AND NOT AS A BOX.
         * Drawn with the same technique as `Patch.astro` — radial gradients rather than
         * pseudo-elements, one declaration for four dots, and the same 1.7px→1.8px
         * antialiasing band, because a hard stop renders a stepped circle at this size. The
         * INSET is 9px where the wall uses 7px, because this bib's padding is larger; a
         * previous version of this comment claimed they matched, and they never did.
         *
         * THEY ARE PAINTED, NOT PUNCHED, AND THAT IS A DEPARTURE WORTH STATING. On the wall
         * a hole is a VOID: `--hole` is the card colour showing through an opaque ink face,
         * and `.bib--booked` drops the holes precisely because a bib with no face has
         * nothing to punch. That argument is right, and following it here was the first
         * draft of this page — which I built, screenshotted and threw away. Alone on the
         * page background an unpunched outline reads as a rectangle with a number in it,
         * which is the generic 404 this design exists to avoid. So the holes are drawn as
         * the EDGE of the punch, in the same hairline the border uses, rather than as the
         * void: same object, different rendering, because the ground changed. Using
         * `--background` as the fill instead was measured and rejected — #FAFAFA on #FAFAFA
         * light and #111111 on #111111 dark, four invisible dots.
         */
        background-image:
            radial-gradient(circle at 9px 9px, color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 32%, transparent) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at calc(100% - 9px) 9px, color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 32%, transparent) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at 9px calc(100% - 9px), color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 32%, transparent) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px),
            radial-gradient(circle at calc(100% - 9px) calc(100% - 9px), color-mix(in srgb, var(--text) 32%, transparent) 0 1.7px, transparent 1.8px);
    }

    /*
     * THE NUMERAL WEARS THE BIB'S DISPLAY METRIC — weight 800, line-height 0.82, the
     * -0.045em tracking — because that treatment was designed for a number printed on a
     * bib, and this is one. Only the SIZE differs, and it has to: on the wall a bib is one
     * cell of a six-item grid, and here it is alone on the page.
     *
     * `min(6rem, 34cqi)` IS A CAP AND A FIT, NOT A FLOOR, and an earlier version of this
     * comment had that backwards — it called the rem "a floor the reader's text size moves",
     * which is not what `min()` does. `6rem` is the CEILING; `34cqi` is what keeps three
     * digits inside the bib, and on a narrow viewport it is the term that binds.
     *
     * THE CONSEQUENCE, MEASURED BY A REVIEW PANEL RATHER THAN GUESSED: on a 320px viewport at
     * 200% text the numeral goes from 86.4px to 64.6px. It gets SMALLER as the reader asks
     * for bigger text, because the bib's own rem padding eats the container that `cqi`
     * resolves against. That is accepted rather than fixed. The alternative is a floor in
     * rem, which at a large root overflows the bib and eventually the viewport — trading a
     * numeral that is merely less enormous for one that clips. Nothing else on the page
     * shrinks: the heading, the lede and both links all grow normally, and at that setting
     * this numeral is still by far the largest thing on screen.
     *
     * NO `tabular-nums`, WHICH USED TO BE HERE WITH A JUSTIFICATION THAT WAS SIMPLY WRONG.
     * The comment claimed proportional figures "set a lonely `1`-width gap between the two
     * zeroes" — but "404" contains no `1`, and it has one zero, not two. Measured effect on
     * this exact string in this font stack: 0.86px out of 178px, 0.48%, on a number that is
     * never column-aligned with another. Removed rather than re-argued; there is no third
     * digit coming.
     */
    .dns-number {
        display: block;
        font-size: min(6rem, 34cqi);
        font-weight: 800;
        line-height: 0.82;
        letter-spacing: -0.045em;
    }
</style>
